Subject: iOS online: touch and gyro look ride the command's mdx/mdy (protocol 33)

DarkPad overlay 0089. The touch layer's look (inputIosLookAddDegrees, 0015)
is latched once per presented frame and read as part of
inputMouseGetScaledDelta. Online that read happens in the stage loop's tick
passes, so a frame drawn between ticks (present-only, above 60 Hz) dropped its
degrees and a frame that ran several catch-up ticks applied them to each.

Now, like the mouse (net.c's s_NetMouseDX):
- netInputAccumulateMouse also sums each frame's touch degrees;
- a stage-loop read returns the sum, and nothing on a present-only pass;
- netTickEnd and netStageStart clear it;
- netpredict's between-ticks view turn includes the unspent degrees.

The degrees go out inside the command's existing f32 mdx/mdy, which the host
multiplies by 3.5 exactly as the client does, so the wire is unchanged and
DarkPad plays with desktop Dab's Mod v3.15.1 peers. The host reads mdx/mdy
only with NETCMD_MOUSELOCKED, which an iPad with no mouse never sets, so
DarkPad's commands always carry it: mdx/mdy hold only look.

Limit: the host's absolute-delta reader (the active menu) multiplies a remote
command by the sign of the client's mouse speed. DarkPad keeps mouse speeds
positive, so touch look keeps its sign. This replaces the earlier
experimental protocol 0x4431, which carried touch separately.

Offline nothing changes. Everything is inside PLATFORM_IOS.

